<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malatesta family</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1295">1295</a>–<a href="/years/1528">1528</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as House of Malatesta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malatesta were an Italian noble family who ruled Rimini from 1295 and at times Pesaro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fano">Fano</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesena">Cesena</a> and other towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> as lords and papal vicars. Their best-known members include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatesta-da-verucchio">Malatesta da Verucchio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismondo-pandolfo-malatesta">Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta</a>, patron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tempio-malatestiano">Tempio Malatestiano</a>. Papal pressure and Venetian rivalry ended their rule in the early 16th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malatesta-family.jpg" alt="Malatesta family" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MostEpic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_house_of_Malatesta.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
